00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.189
BETH: The purpose of a risk assessment
is to evaluate risks or hazards, then to

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.530
remove that hazard or minimise the level
of risk by adding measures to control it.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.719
JOE: Any event or activity can have an
element of risk to it, so it’s important

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.590
to assess any risks ahead of time and be
prepared for those risks to happen whilst

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.990
minimising the chance of them happening.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:26.620
Establishing what actions can
be taken can help to minimise

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.450
the impact if it does happen.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.449
Planning should help to prioritise
risks that are more likely

00:00:32.519 --> 00:00:34.639
happen and cause greater impact.

00:00:35.029 --> 00:00:38.490
BETH: When looking at risk assessment
that involves an individual with autism,

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.400
this can involve specific new activities,
or more routine everyday things.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.230
It may be needed for a day out or
a school trip, an event, activity

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.819
or a holiday, or something really
simple like preparing a meal.

00:00:52.529 --> 00:00:55.809
JOE: Usual methods of risk assessment
are made up of structured forms and

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:00.250
charts documenting any prospective
activity and the problems associated

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.400
with it such as health and safety issues.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.060
However, this doesn’t always take
into consideration the person,

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:08.490
their needs, objectives and dreams.

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:11.690
BETH: When it comes to looking
specifically at-risk assessment

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.660
focused on activities individuals
with autism might do daily, then more

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.490
consideration needs to be given to
how the ‘risks’ can be outweighed by

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.390
the benefits of taking those risks.

00:01:22.309 --> 00:01:26.520
JOE: A risk assessment can be completed
as part of other assessments to assess

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.824
the environment both physical and social
for any risks to an individual with

00:01:30.939 --> 00:01:33.080
autism, taking a holistic approach.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.259
BETH: Risk assessment can give an insight
into the level of risk an individual will

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.610
face during any activity within a home,
educational or work environment, but

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.940
can be more specifically focused on new
risks or new challenges going forward.

00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:51.590
It can also consider how an individual’s
behaviour within the situation or

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.830
activity may affect the outcome.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.750
JOE: Individuals with autism are often
so single-minded about their interests

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.850
that they are unaware of the effect
that their actions may have on others,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.740
or any consequences to themselves.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:09.340
Therefore, risk assessments need to
not just focus on the immediate health

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.990
and safety risks, but the overall
well-being of an individual going

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:17.710
forward long-term, and the benefits that
can be gained by positive risk taking.

